# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: The Reed Settles After the Second Kick

**Story & Context:** The debut opens at 136 BPM with Ellis programming a two-step pattern whose second kick lands slightly before Aoi’s first hichiriki note reaches stable pitch. The note begins sharp, bends downward, and locks just after the kick. On the next cycle Ellis withholds the bass until the exact lock point, making the groove appear to lean backward without changing tempo.

Mina enters every fourth cycle with one prepared-Rhodes open fifth after the reed has stabilized. Ren replaces the main clap with a single dry shime-daiko stroke on alternate phrases. Naomi waits until Aoi stops completely, then adds a rounded low fifth that lasts less than one bar.

At the midpoint, Aoi intentionally changes reeds. The new reed settles faster, so the bass response arrives earlier even though the sequence itself has not been rewritten metrically. The final phrase begins with the expected unstable note but cuts off before pitch lock; Ellis therefore withholds the final sub response entirely.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary image of a hichiriki reed beside a hand-marked rhythm sheet showing two kick symbols and a later pitch-lock point, red lacquer sample board and Rhodes key in shallow focus, warm freight-platform lamp. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “second kick,” “not stable yet,” “wait for the lock,” and “no bass if it never settles.”

## Track 2: Vermilion Gate Opens One Swing Late

**Story & Context:** The second track runs at 134 BPM and focuses on delayed harmonic response. Aoi plays a three-note hichiriki call ending on a flattened seventh with an exaggerated upward scoop. Mina does not answer the written note; she waits until the scoop stops and then strikes a prepared-Rhodes minor seventh one swung sixteenth later.

Ellis writes a short bassline that uses only tonic and fifth, but alternates whether each note comes before or after the hichiriki settles. Ren places shime-daiko strokes only on the cycles where the bass arrives late. Naomi contributes two-note flugelhorn falls after every eighth bar, creating a warm low-mid release beneath the dry upper reed.

During the final section, the drums remain unchanged while Aoi shortens each attack. The groove seems to accelerate because the response events occur sooner relative to the bar. The track ends with one unmuffled Rhodes chord after all bass has stopped.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Large vermilion freight gate half-open inside a dark loading corridor, one swing-grid diagram chalked onto the concrete, hichiriki and prepared Rhodes reflected in a black lacquer panel. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “one swing late,” “gate opening,” “settle then answer,” and “leave the chord dry.”

## Track 3: Platform Lamp Goes Dark Before the Note Locks

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 138 BPM with the sparsest drums on the EP: kick, one short snare, and occasional hat pair. Aoi sustains longer hichiriki notes than elsewhere on the record, allowing the unstable attack phase to become more obvious. Ellis writes sub notes only after lock, so the first minute contains unusually long low-frequency gaps.

Ren gradually replaces electronic snares with shime-daiko until the acoustic drum owns every backbeat. Mina reduces her voicings from two-note stabs to isolated Rhodes notes. Naomi shifts from pitched brass responses to breath-heavy air attacks with almost no stable tone.

In the final minute, an old platform lamp in the depot is switched off manually after each reed attack but before pitch lock. One by one, the visual cues disappear while the musical responses continue. On the final note, the lamp goes dark, Aoi’s pitch never fully stabilizes, and the ensemble remains silent. Only the reed breath and short room reflection are left.

